From 4edb9e2a87750f564bb7c8ebad2add68797411a7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 06:06:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] fix(auth): disabled Codex/xAI accounts are refused
 at the singleton resolvers; slot rows match by account

Second review (exact head e9f591f9f25) found:
- resolve_codex_runtime_credentials / resolve_xai_oauth_runtime_credentials handed a disabled
  account's token to their other callers (plugin provider requests, usage fetch, aux forced
  refresh, xAI aux) and spent its refresh grant first. Both now refuse before any refresh
  (status/read-only lookups still report).
- the Codex singleton row's id is its slot and its label is the generic 'device_code', so
  disabling it disabled whoever signed in next. Records now carry the JWT account (email or
  ChatGPT account id); a slot row whose token names another account never matches, generic
  labels never match by label, and the account stays disabled wherever it reappears.
- API-key providers fall back to env vars / any pool row when select() is empty, so
  'hermes auth disable' is now limited to anthropic, openai-codex, xai-oauth (the panel's scope).
- the delegated-child endpoint fallback could bind a disabled row; _is_sole_credential counted
  disabled rows.
- token_disabled reads persisted rows (no load_pool seeding re-entry) and checks refresh tokens;
  disabled_keys fails soft when no store path resolves.

Proof: 6 new tests in test_credential_pool_disabled_paths.py RED with the fixes reverted, GREEN
with them; reviewer repros (codex singleton fresh/expiring, slot reuse) now refuse / pass.
